        "dependency_level": 6,
        "statement": "Define the standard figure-eight knot $K_8$ to be the oriented closure of the three-strand geometric braid $(\\sigma_1\\sigma_2^{-1})^2$. Its endpoint permutation is a $3$-cycle, so this closure has one component. Let $\\Delta_0=[0,1,2,3]$ and $\\Delta_1=[0',1',2',3']$ be oriented ideal tetrahedra with orientation-reversing affine face maps $$ [0,1,2]\\to[3',1',2'],\\quad [0,1,3]\\to[0',1',2'],\\quad [0,2,3]\\to[0',1',3'],\\quad [1,2,3]\\to[0',2',3']. $$ This is the two-tetrahedron ideal triangulation obtained from the standard four-crossing figure-eight diagram by cutting along crossing arcs, identifying upper and lower polyhedron faces with equal region labels, and collapsing the two bigon faces. Its edge classes are $$ \\{01,02,23,0'1',1'3',2'3'\\},\\qquad \\{12,03,13,1'2',0'2',0'3'\\}; $$ each has valence six, and the unique ideal vertex link is a torus. Put shape $z_1$ on $01,23$, shape $z_2$ on $03,12$, and shape $z_3$ on $02,13$ in $\\Delta_0$; put shape $w_1$ on $0'1',2'3'$, shape $w_2$ on $0'3',1'2'$, and shape $w_3$ on $0'2',1'3'$ in $\\Delta_1$. Unfold the eight cusp-link triangles by the face maps into the standard rectangular strip, and label them $a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h$ from left to right; identify the strip boundary sides as prescribed by those same maps. Let $\\alpha$ be the edge path across the strip from the left side of $a$ on the left boundary to the left side of $a$ on the right boundary, and let $\\beta$ be the edge path on the left boundary from the base of $a$ to the top of $h$. Their ordered corner factors are respectively $z_3,w_2^{-1},z_2,w_3^{-1},z_3,w_2^{-1},z_2,w_3^{-1}$ and $z_2^{-1},w_1$. The face-pairing incidence check gives one positive transverse intersection, so their homology classes form a basis of $H_1$ and the loops form a basis of $\\pi_1$ of the torus link. The two edge products are $z_1^2z_3w_1^2w_3$ and $z_2^2z_3w_2^2w_3$, while $$ H(\\alpha)=\\left(\\frac{z_2z_3}{w_2w_3}\\right)^2,\\qquad H(\\beta)=\\frac{w_1}{z_2}. $$ For $\\omega=e^{i\\pi/3}$, assigning $z_1=z_2=z_3=w_1=w_2=w_3=\\omega$ makes both edge products and both cusp multipliers equal to $1$ and gives total angle $2\\pi$ around each edge. The gluing therefore gives the figure-eight complement a complete finite-volume curvature $-1$ metric with one peripheral torus cusp.",
