        "statement": "Every compact exterior of a smooth knot in $S^3$ is non-Seifert-fibered. Counterexample: Let $S^3=\\{(z,w)\\in\\mathbb C^2:|z|^2+|w|^2=1\\}$ with the weighted circle action $g_\\theta(z,w)=(e^{3i\\theta}z,e^{2i\\theta}w)$, and let $K(t)=2^{-1/2}(e^{3it},e^{2it})$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$. The Clifford torus $|z|=|w|=2^{-1/2}$ separates $S^3$ into two solid tori; $K$ winds twice around the core of $|z|\\le|w|$ and three times around the core of $|w|\\le|z|$, so it is the standard $(3,2)$ torus knot, the trefoil. Let $q:S^3\\to S^3/S^1$ be the orbit map and choose a small closed disk $D$ about $q(K)$ disjoint from the axis orbits. Then $N(K)=q^{-1}(D)$ is an invariant solid torus neighborhood and $E_K=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ is Seifert-fibered over a disk with cone points of orders $2$ and $3$; a regular fiber is central in $\\pi_1(E_K)$.",
